        "title": "Entire and Meromorphic Functions",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Entire functions (holomorphic on all of ℂ) have no singularities in the finite plane — they can only grow toward infinity. Meromorphic functions allow poles. The theory of entire functions describes how they can grow and how their zeros are distributed. The key insight is that you can *prescribe* the zeros of an entire function (within constraints) and build the function from its zero set via infinite products. The Hadamard factorization theorem does this for functions of finite order, making it the entire-function analogue of partial-fraction decomposition for rational functions."
    }
